    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d381">

                <pb facs="0746" n="730" sameAs="frus:frus1950v02/pg730"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.10/8–950: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Griffis</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted   priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-08-09T17:00:00-03:00">August 9, 1950—5
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>111. For Miller and Tewksbury from Ambassador. Delay implementing Export Import
                    credit is becoming painfully and cumulatively embarrassing here. Can Department
                    take whatever action is necessary <pb facs="0747" n="731" xml:id="pg731"/> to
                    immediately end suspense and advise us if there is anything doable here.</p>
                <p>British and Argentine deadlock on meat prices resulting in discontinuance of
                    shipments and complete jam refrigerator space with probable necessity packers
                    discontinuance purchasing livestock threatens demoralization and probable
                    terrific financial losses American packers here. Could the Department consider
                    unofficial intervention British obvious that violent break England and Argentina
                    this time extremely dangerous international solidarity.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d381-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Griffis</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d381-f1" n="1" xml:id="d381-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram 103, August 15, 1950, the Department replied that on August 11 the
                    British Embassy had been consulted. “Dept merely acquainted Brit your and Dept’s
                    view seriousness matter, since undesirable intervene even unofficially at this
                    time. However evident from conversation that Brit still maintain adamant
                    position. Recognizing fluidity relationship meat problem to world events,
                    problem will be kept active for reappraisal when and as desirable.”
                    (835.10/8–950)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
